=== tool delegate
Run a task in a sub-thread that has your tools and works in this folder. Use it only when the person asks you to delegate, to use sub-agents, or to work on several things at once; never on your own initiative, and not for a new thread the person asks for.
- The sub-thread does not see this conversation: put everything it needs in `task`.
- The call waits and returns the sub-thread's final answer; several calls in one reply run at once. With `background`, it returns at once and you get a message when the sub-thread finishes.
- Every result names the sub-thread; to give it more work, pass its id in `thread`.
- Its answer is not proof: check what it changed before you report the work done.
- The person sees every sub-thread and can steer it.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "background": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Return at once; you get a message when the sub-thread finishes. Do not wait for it or poll.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "task": {
      "description": "The complete task. A new sub-thread knows nothing else.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "thread": {
      "description": "Id of one of your sub-threads, running or finished, to continue; it keeps its earlier work. Omit to start a new\none.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "title": {
      "description": "A short title for a new sub-thread, shown to the person.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "task"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool edit
Replace text in a file. `old` must match exactly one place, or every place with `all`; include just enough lines to make it unique. Matching tries the exact text, then with typographic characters (curly quotes, dashes, special spaces) read as plain ones, then line by line ignoring trailing whitespace; an inexact match is noted. Returns the change as a unified diff. If `old` is not found, the closest lines in the file are shown, or a note says `new` is already there.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "all": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Replace every match.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "new": {
      "description": "Replacement text.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "old": {
      "description": "Text to replace, exactly as read returned it.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "path": {
      "description": "File: relative to the workspace, absolute, or starting with ~ for the home folder.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path",
    "old",
    "new"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool execute
Run JavaScript as the body of an async function, with your tools on `tools`: `await tools.read({ path: "a.txt" })` returns the tool's text result and rejects on failure. Use loops, Promise.all and code to work on the results. Returns the script's return value as JSON and anything written with console.log. Use it for work of many steps: reading or changing many files, many browser actions, batch extraction. `tools` has each of your tools by name, except execute and tools that ask the person or start threads, plus the tools listed in <catalog>.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "code": {
      "description": "JavaScript run as the body of an async function; its return value is the result.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "code"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool glob
Find files under a directory whose paths match a glob, respecting ignore files. Returns one path per line, newest first, 200 per page; a note gives the offset of the next page.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "offset": {
      "description": "First result to return, from 1, newest first.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "default": "",
      "description": "Directory to search: relative to the workspace, absolute, or starting with ~ for the home folder. Defaults to\nthe workspace.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "pattern": {
      "description": "Glob pattern, such as src/**/*.rs.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "pattern"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool grep
Search file contents with a regular expression (ripgrep syntax), in the files under a directory or in one file, respecting ignore files. Returns matches as `path:line: text` and context lines as `path:line- text`, 250 matches per page; a note gives the offset of the next page. Lines over 500 characters are truncated. Binary files are skipped and counted.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "case_insensitive": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Ignore case.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "context": {
      "default": 0,
      "description": "Lines of context to show around each match.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 0,
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "glob": {
      "description": "Search only files matching this glob; a glob without / matches file names.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "literal": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Treat `pattern` as plain text.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "offset": {
      "description": "First match to return, from 1, files ordered by path.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "default": "",
      "description": "Directory or file to search: relative to the workspace, absolute, or starting with ~ for the home folder.\nDefaults to the workspace.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "pattern": {
      "description": "Regular expression in ripgrep syntax, or plain text with `literal`.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "pattern"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool question
Ask the person one to eight questions at once. A question can offer options to choose one of, or several with `multiple`, or no options for a free-text answer. The person may answer in their own words, skip questions, or dismiss them all. Put your recommended option first and add "(recommended)" to its label. Ask only what you cannot find out yourself and what changes what you do.
--- parameters
{
  "$defs": {
    "Asked": {
      "description": "A question.",
      "properties": {
        "header": {
          "description": "A label of one or two words, shown above the question.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "multiple": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Allow choosing several options.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "options": {
          "description": "Options to choose from; omit for a free-text answer.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/Choice"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "question": {
          "description": "The question, as the person will read it.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "question"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "Choice": {
      "description": "An option.",
      "properties": {
        "description": {
          "description": "What the option means, where the label is not enough.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "label": {
          "description": "The option, in a few words.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "label"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  },
  "properties": {
    "questions": {
      "description": "One to eight questions.",
      "items": {
        "$ref": "#/$defs/Asked"
      },
      "maxItems": 8,
      "minItems": 1,
      "type": "array"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "questions"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool read
Read a file, or list a directory (directory names end in /). Text is returned as is, without line numbers, up to 2000 lines or 48 KB per call; lines over 2000 characters are truncated. A note after the text gives the lines returned and the offset to continue from. Images are shown to you; PDFs and other binary files are named, not shown.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "limit": {
      "description": "Number of lines or entries to return.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "offset": {
      "description": "First line or entry to return, from 1.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "description": "File or directory: relative to the workspace, absolute, or starting with ~ for the home folder.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool shell
Run a bash command in the workspace (no need to cd there). Its input is closed. Returns stdout and stderr together, then how it ended: [exit N], a signal, or a timeout. The time limit is 120 seconds; set `timeout_seconds` for up to 600. For reading, searching and editing files, use read, grep, glob and edit instead. In default mode commands run in a sandbox that can write only the workspace, the memory folder, the scratchpad and the temporary folders; to write anywhere else (such as a global install), run the command again with `escalate` and a `justification`, and the person decides.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "command": {
      "description": "The command to run.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "escalate": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Run outside the sandbox, once the person approves.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "justification": {
      "description": "Why it must write outside the sandbox, in one sentence for the person.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "timeout_seconds": {
      "description": "Time limit in seconds; 120 by default, at most 600.",
      "format": "uint64",
      "maximum": 600,
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "command"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool terminal
Work the thread's terminals, which the person sees beside the chat.
- open: run a command that does not exit by itself (a server, a watcher) in a new terminal in the workspace, sandboxed as shell commands are. Returns the terminal's number and its screen after a moment. When the command exits you get a message with how it ended and its last screen; do not poll for it.
- read: return what a terminal shows, the person's terminals included.
- write: type into a terminal you opened (`\u0003` for Ctrl-C, `\r` for Enter); returns the screen after.
- close: close a terminal you opened, ending its command.
For a command that exits by itself, use shell. Link a terminal in your reply as :terminal{id=<number>}, or as ::terminal{id=<number>} on its own line to show its card with its last lines.
--- parameters
{
  "oneOf": [
    {
      "description": "Run a command in a new terminal.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "open",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "command": {
          "description": "The command to run.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "escalate": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Run outside the sandbox, once the person approves.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "justification": {
          "description": "Why it must write outside the sandbox, in one sentence for the person.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "command"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Return what a terminal shows, the person's terminals included.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "read",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Type into a terminal you opened.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "write",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        },
        "text": {
          "description": "Keys as text: `\\u0003` for Ctrl-C, `\\r` for Enter.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal",
        "text"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Close a terminal you opened, ending its command.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "close",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  ]
}

=== tool thread
Work with the person's other threads, which they see in their thread list.
- start: create a thread in this folder with your model and mode; `message` is its first input.
- send: send a thread a message; it takes it at its next step, or starts a turn with it.
- stop: stop the turn a thread is working on.
Use it only when the person asks for a new thread, or names a thread to message or stop; never for sub-agents, delegation or parallel work. A new thread does not see this conversation: put everything it needs in `message`. With `report`, you get a message when the turn that takes your message ends; do not wait for it or poll. Link a thread in your reply as :thread{id=<id>}, or as ::thread{id=<id>} on its own line to show its card.
--- parameters
{
  "oneOf": [
    {
      "description": "Create a thread in this folder with your model and mode; `message` is its first input.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "start",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "message": {
          "description": "Everything the new thread needs; it does not see this conversation.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "report": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Get a message when the turn that takes `message` ends.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "title": {
          "description": "A short title, shown to the person.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "message"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Send a thread a message; it takes it at its next step, or starts a turn with it.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "send",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "message": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "report": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Get a message when the turn that takes `message` ends.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "thread": {
          "description": "The thread's id.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "thread",
        "message"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Stop the turn a thread is working on.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "stop",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "thread": {
          "description": "The thread's id.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "thread"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  ]
}

=== tool write
Write a whole file, creating it and any missing directories. Returns how many lines were added and removed. To change part of a file, use edit.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "content": {
      "description": "The file's complete new contents.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "path": {
      "description": "File: relative to the workspace, absolute, or starting with ~ for the home folder.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path",
    "content"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}
